<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <PropertyGroup>
        <Title>Stride Community Toolkit - Effects</Title>
        <Description>Toolkit pieces that come with a shader of their own: post-processing colour transforms and GPU picking.

What's inside:
- PostProcessing.NightVision: luminance amplified into a green image with a soft white bloom, film grain, scanlines and a vignette
- PostProcessing.Thermal: luminance mapped onto a false-colour ramp from cold blue through red to white-hot
- Picking.GpuPicker: what is under a screen point, read from an id pass over the rendered meshes - no colliders needed; entity, mesh, material, instance index and hit point

Each colour transform is a ColorTransform with one small shader; add it to a PostProcessingEffects' ColorTransforms the way the engine's own Vignetting or FilmGrain are added.$(StrideVersionNote)</Description>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <Import Project="..\CommonSettings.props" />

    <ItemGroup>
        <!-- This library ships shaders. Shaders are assets compiled by the asset compiler according
             to the Graphics API target, so the package that owns the shader owns the dependency.
             Its targets also stage Effects/ into the package as stride/Assets/Effects and generate
             the matching .sdpkg manifest - do not hand-write either. -->
        <PackageReference Include="Stride.AssetCompiler" Version="$(StrideVersion)" IncludeAssets="build;buildTransitive" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="..\Stride.CommunityToolkit\Stride.CommunityToolkit.csproj" />
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>